BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE:Santa Rosa Junior College
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261220
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261221
LOCATION:Multicultural Museum - Santa Rosa Campus
SUMMARY:Standing in Her Power: Portraits Beyond the Colonial Frame
DESCRIPTION:Spanning ages 20 to 81\, the women featured in the exhibition
  represent a diversity of lives\, identities and experiences that challen
 ge any single representation of Indigenous womanhood. They are land defen
 ders\, language keepers\, dancers\, bead workers\, mothers\, daughters an
 d artists\, each with their own relationship to culture\, community\, lan
 d and identity.  Julian Cook\, an SRJC anthropology student and co-creato
 r of the project\, conceived the exhibition shortly after she began worki
 ng at the SRJC Multicultural Museum. While cataloging the museum's book c
 ollection\, Cook came across Edward S. Curtis: The Women\, a collection o
 f photographs that\, despite being shaped by romanticized notions of Nati
 ve peoples as a "vanishing race\," also captured the dignity and everyday
  lives of Native women.  "I don't want to replace one narrow image of Nat
 ive womanhood with another\," Cook explains. "I want to challenge the ide
 a that there is any single look of a Native woman\, and to give these wom
 en the opportunity to decide for themselves how they wanted to be seen." 
  To bring that vision to life\, the museum collaborated with San Francisc
 o-based Mexican American photographer Alexa "LexMex" Treviño (https://ww
 w.lexmexart.com/about)\, whose work explores cultural identity\, communit
 y and representation through portraiture. Her previous projects include C
 orazón de la Misión\, a series of monumental public portraits honoring 
 members of San Francisco's Latinx community.  Each woman featured in Stan
 ding in Her Power exercised complete creative control over her portrait\,
  choosing how to present herself rather than conforming to someone else's
  expectations of what a Native woman should look like. The accompanying s
 tories offer a more personal perspective on the connections between ident
 ity\, culture\, ancestry\, and community.This project is funded by a gran
 t from the Santa Rosa Junior College Foundation Randolph Newman Cultural 
 Enrichment Endowment.  For more information\, visit the SRJC Multicultura
 l Museum website (https://museum.santarosa.edu/event/standing-her-power-p
 ortraits-beyond-colonial-frame).
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  Spanning ages 20 to 81\, the women feat
 ured in the exhibition represent a diversity of lives\, identities and ex
 periences that challenge any single representation of Indigenous womanhoo
 d. They are land defenders\, language keepers\, dancers\, bead workers\, 
 mothers\, daughters and artists\, each with their own relationship to cul
 ture\, community\, land and identity.</p><p>  Julian Cook\, an SRJC anthr
 opology student and co-creator of the project\, conceived the exhibition 
 shortly after she began working at the SRJC Multicultural Museum. While c
 ataloging the museum's book collection\, Cook came across <em>Edward S. C
 urtis: The Women</em>\, a collection of photographs that\, despite being 
 shaped by romanticized notions of Native peoples as a "vanishing race\," 
 also captured the dignity and everyday lives of Native women.</p><p>  "I 
 don't want to replace one narrow image of Native womanhood with another\,
 " Cook explains. "I want to challenge the idea that there is any single l
 ook of a Native woman\, and to give these women the opportunity to decide
  for themselves how they wanted to be seen."</p><p>  To bring that vision
  to life\, the museum collaborated with San Francisco-based Mexican Ameri
 can photographer <a href="https://www.lexmexart.com/about" target="_blank
 " rel="noopener">Alexa "LexMex" Treviño</a>\, whose work explores cultur
 al identity\, community and representation through portraiture. Her previ
 ous projects include <em>Corazón de la Misión</em>\, a series of monume
 ntal public portraits honoring members of San Francisco's Latinx communit
 y.</p><p>  Each woman featured in <em>Standing in Her Power</em> exercise
 d complete creative control over her portrait\, choosing how to present h
 erself rather than conforming to someone else's expectations of what a Na
 tive woman should look like. The accompanying stories offer a more person
 al perspective on the connections between identity\, culture\, ancestry\,
  and community.</p><p><em>This project is funded by a grant from the Sant
 a Rosa Junior College Foundation Randolph Newman Cultural Enrichment Endo
 wment.</em></p><p>  For more information\, visit the <a href="https://mus
 eum.santarosa.edu/event/standing-her-power-portraits-beyond-colonial-fram
 e" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SRJC Multicultural Museum website</a>.<
 /p>
